  Third question, and it's the structural one. "Other forms will need it later" means the identity has to be importable
  from somewhere, but this repo has no way to share code in the browser today — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic [38;5;153m<script src>[39m with
  everything at top level, and [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m that no browser can load and no bundler processes. A second
  form can't reach a session store until that's settled.

  - [1mNative ES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, with a new [38;5;153msrc/session.js[39m exporting [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m /
    [38;5;153msetSession()[39m / [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m. Buys you real import boundaries with no build step, and later forms just [38;5;153mimport[39m.
    Costs: modules are fetched with CORS rules, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly from the filesystem stops working — you'd
    need to serve the directory ([38;5;153mnpx serve[39m, [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m). That's the one habit this changes.
  - [1mA global namespace object[0m — [38;5;153mwindow.Session = {...}[39m from a plain [38;5;153m<script src="src/session.js">[39m loaded before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
    Buys you zero disruption; [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working. Costs: load order becomes load-bearing, the store is mutable by
    anything on the page, and it's not testable without a DOM. Migrating to modules later touches every consumer — so
    the later forms make this more expensive, not less.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — real imports plus [38;5;153mfile://[39m compatibility, at the cost of a toolchain, a [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m, and a build
    step in a repo whose [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no dependencies at all. YAGNI for two forms.

  [1mMy recommendation: native ES modules.[0m It's the option that makes "other forms will need it later" a one-line import
  instead of a load-order problem, and it costs you one command to serve the page instead of double-clicking the file.
  The global approach is the one that gets more expensive with exactly the growth you described.
